  "finding": "Two results. (1) The MOTION channel does NOT discriminate and is unusable as a reactivity signal, two ways. By point estimate it is large and positive for EVERY column -- surrogate +0.34, talking-heads +0.35..+0.78, GT +0.92, dyadic +0.37..+1.19 (x1e4) -- and the ordering is scrambled: talking-heads score as high as or above genuine dyadic seeds (e.g. SoulX Pro +0.78 > AVTR-1 seed1 +0.40), so motion magnitude does not rank real listeners above non-reactive ones. By significance its identity CIs are wide and NOTHING is significant, GT included (p=0.056); surrogate p=0.12, talking-heads p>=0.08. So a metric read off visible motion would both mis-rank and certify nothing. Only the AUDIO channel separates: GT (p=0.003) and the dyadic models >0, surrogate and talking-heads ~0 and n.s. The conditional aud|mot IS the audio-specific definition, not a bolt-on. (2) On SI-184 aud|mot == marginal aud (|delta|<=0.03): audio and motion are near-orthogonal in the reaction (V(both)~V(aud)+V(mot), seen in aud|mot~=aud AND mot|aud~=mot), so partialling motion out is a no-op for the NUMBER here -- robustness, and free insurance where audio and motion are collinear. The pre-registered prediction (marginal inflates a control) was NOT observed; the justification is the motion channel's non-discrimination. Baseline aud|mot kept (rubric rule 4). See CVPR_zeroother_design.md 13.1."
}